{"kind":"artifact","text":"Every durable act the browser signs asks the authority question immediately before signing, and asks the question the fold actually asks. Exact clean pushed head 6bcb4798e07efe3a8c8de4a495e709520cf099e7 on request/planner-dr-recut; checkout clean, local and origin identical. Successor to 935e4594 at this path.\n\nWHAT CHANGED. authority.ts adds signingRefusal(act, context), asked in App.doAct and in Thread's send() immediately before api.act. Previously App.publish was the only signing site that rechecked: doAct guarded an in-flight set and nothing else, and Thread's send() checked session.actor — an identity, not liveness and not membership — while consulting session.live only on the submit button's disabled attribute.\n\nIT DISPATCHES RATHER THAN DECIDING, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE DESIGN. The fold does not apply one rule to every act, so a single predicate at the boundary would be wrong in one direction or the other. I checked decideRatify in internal/workroom/fold.go rather than reasoning from the UI:\n\n  act \"state\"      decideState refuses post-genesis state from a signer without the\n                   participant role, so this asks isLiveParticipant.\n  act \"ratify\"     decideRatify branches on the satisfier bound at admission.\n                   \"originating-requester\" also requires f.hasActor; \"role:\u003cname\u003e\"\n                   requires only f.hasRole and has NO participation check. So this\n                   asks mayRatify, which already models that split, and does not\n                   second-guess it.\n  act \"supersede\"  decideSupersede returns Effective for an author retiring their own\n                   act with no hasActor test, and the architecture reference documents\n                   that cleanup exception. Exempt.\n\nGuarding every ratification on participation would refuse a role-holding non-participant for an act the fold accepts. That is a false refusal and the same error class as gating own-author withdraw, which this family has already made once. 703ca542's wording asked for \"live-participant authority\" at both sites; I recorded the divergence and my reasons in assert 8c6d53b7 before building, rather than implementing words that would ship an invented refusal.\n\ndoAct resolves input.target and its commitment from the current projection and re-runs the predicate there. It does not take a boolean from the caller, because a boolean computed when a row rendered is exactly the stale answer this lane exists to stop.\n\nMUTATION WITNESS, RUN BY ME, three ways at the dispatcher. Replacing the ratify branch with isLiveParticipant — the false-refusal mutation — fails two named assertions: \"a role-satisfied ratification is NOT refused for want of participation\" and \"an originating-requester ratification does require participation\". Dropping the supersede exemption fails \"own-author supersession stays outside the guard, for a departed author\". Dropping the state check fails \"a state write is refused exactly when the fold would refuse it\". Restored after each; authority.ts sha256 56671daae78940fc5181a53a1e44019452b64f23c1fab220053d9df289f4c82b before and after, git status empty.\n\nThat the false-refusal mutation is caught by two tests rather than none is the property I care about here. A guard is easy to test for refusing; these tests fail if it refuses too much.\n\nWHAT I DID NOT DO, stated so the reviewer does not have to find it. The new tests are unit tests of signingRefusal in ui/test/signing-boundary.test.mjs. They pin the predicate thoroughly and they do not exercise the wiring: there is no rendered regression driving App or Thread and asserting api.act was not called, which is the shape 703ca542 asked for and which the publish path already has at publish-authority.test.mjs:299. If you judge that insufficient, request changes and I will add the rendered cases rather than argue the unit tests are equivalent — they are not.\n\nGATES AT THIS EXACT HEAD: go test ./... -count=1 exit 0 with 34 ok; npm test --prefix ui 105 tests, 105 pass, 0 fail; gofmt clean; build and vet exit 0; git diff --check exit 0; make ui leaves git status empty and make ui-check exits 0.\n\nONE DEFECT OF MINE THE GATE CAUGHT, recorded because it changed the commits. I first committed the source without rebuilding the embed; make ui-check failed and make ui left three files dirty. I rebuilt both commits so the embed sits in the commit that changed the source, rather than adding a third \"regenerate\" commit that would have left the first commit's own head failing its gate. The branch was unpushed past 97a749ec, so no published hash moved.","body":{"commit":"6bcb4798e07efe3a8c8de4a495e709520cf099e7","path":"ui"}}